Supreme People's Court

Several Provisions on Strictly Implementing the Disciplinary System under the Judges Law of the People's Republic of China

(Adopted at the 1276th Session of the Judicial Committee of the Supreme People's Court on June 10, 2003)

Fa Fa [2003] No. 10

Article 1 These Provisions are formulated for the purpose of strictly implementing the disciplinary system under the Judges Law of the People's Republic of China (hereinafter referred to as the Judges Law), further regulating the professional conduct of judges, maintaining strict trial discipline, building a contingent of judges that is politically steadfast, professionally proficient,作风优良, and judicially impartial, and ensuring judicial fairness and efficiency.

Article 2 Judges must faithfully execute the Constitution and laws. If a judge fails to perform duties according to law and commits any of the acts listed in Article 32 of the Judges Law (i.e., the thirteen types of prohibited acts specified in Articles 3 to 15 of these Provisions), he or she shall be subject to legal or disciplinary sanctions.

Article 3 Judges shall love the motherland and the people, support the leadership of the Communist Party of China, uphold the socialist system, and shall not disseminate言论 that damages the reputation of the state, participate in illegal organizations, participate in activities such as assemblies, processions, and demonstrations aimed at opposing the state, or participate in strikes.

Where this Article is violated, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post, and the judge shall be dismissed or expelled.

Article 4 Judges shall be honest and incorruptible, exercise self-discipline and serve the public interest, and shall not engage in corruption or bribery. The following acts are strictly prohibited:

(1) Taking advantage of their position to embezzle, steal, defraud, or illegally占有 litigation fees, enforcement funds and property, case deposits, illegal gains and stolen property and their fruits, and other public property by other means;

(2) Taking advantage of their position to extort property from parties, their agents ad litem, defenders, or clients;

(3) Taking advantage of their position to illegally accept property from parties, their agents ad litem, defenders, or clients, and seeking benefits for them;

(4) Violating state regulations by receiving various forms of kickbacks or handling fees for personal possession.

Where the above provisions are violated, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post, and the judge shall be dismissed or expelled.

Article 5 In adjudicating cases, judges must base their findings on facts and take the law as the criterion, handle cases impartially, and shall not engage in favoritism or perversion of the law. The following acts are strictly prohibited:

(1) In trial and enforcement work, deliberately distorting facts and law to render perverted judgments or decisions;

(2) For personal gain or out of favoritism, showing partiality toward one party and deliberately violating legal provisions to compel the other party to act against their true intentions and relinquish their rights;

(3) Engaging in malpractice for personal gain by granting commutation of sentence, parole, or temporary service of sentence outside prison to a criminal who does not meet the conditions for such measures.

Where the above provisions are violated, the judge shall be given a major demerit or demoted; if the circumstances are serious, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post, and the judge shall be dismissed or given a punishment of removal from office or above.

Article 6 Judges shall respect the personal rights of criminal defendants and shall not extort confessions by torture.

Where this Article is violated, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post, and the judge shall be expelled.

Article 7 Judges shall be faithful to the truth of the facts and shall not conceal evidence or forge evidence. The following acts are strictly prohibited:

(1) Altering, concealing, forging, tampering with, or intentionally destroying evidence;

(2) Using violence, threats, bribery, or other means to prevent witnesses from testifying or to induce others to give false testimony;

(3) Using violence or other illegal means to extort witness testimony.

Where the above provisions are violated, the judge shall be given a major demerit or demoted; if the circumstances are serious, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post, and the judge shall be dismissed or given a punishment of removal from office or above.

Article 8 Judges shall strictly observe the discipline of confidentiality and shall not disclose state secrets or secrets of trial work. The following acts are strictly prohibited:

(1) Disclosing state secrets;

(2) Disclosing the details of a case to parties or their related persons, tipping them off, or otherwise disclosing the specific contents of a case;

(3) Disclosing the specific circumstances and records of collegiate bench deliberations or judicial committee discussions of cases, or other secrets of trial or enforcement work.

Where the above provisions are violated, the judge shall be given a major demerit or demoted; if the circumstances are serious, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post, and the judge shall be dismissed or given a punishment of removal from office or above.

Article 9 Judges shall exercise their powers correctly, protect the litigation rights and civil rights and interests of litigation participants and non-parties according to law, and shall not abuse their powers to infringe upon the lawful rights and interests of natural persons, legal persons, or other organizations. The following acts are strictly prohibited:

(1) Intentionally and illegally infringing upon or depriving parties or other litigation participants of their litigation rights;

(2) Intentionally and illegally adopting preservation measures or compulsory enforcement measures;

(3) Intentionally and illegally adopting compulsory measures such as detention against parties, other litigation participants, or non-parties;

(4) Intentionally and illegally infringing upon the lawful rights and interests of non-parties.

Where the above provisions are violated, the judge shall be given a warning up to a major demerit; if the circumstances are serious, the judge shall be dismissed or given a punishment of demotion or above. Where removal from office or expulsion is imposed, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post.

Article 10 Judges shall be diligent and dedicated to their work, and shall not be derelict of duty, causing erroneous cases or serious losses to parties. The following acts are strictly prohibited:

(1) Gross negligence leading to erroneous judgments or erroneous enforcement;

(2) Gross irresponsibility, failure to perform statutory duties, causing serious losses to the interests of parties or other persons.

Where the above provisions are violated, the judge shall be given a warning up to a major demerit; if serious consequences or major losses are caused, the judge shall be dismissed or given a punishment of demotion or above. Where removal from office or expulsion is imposed, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post.

Article 11 Judges shall strictly comply with the litigation time limits prescribed by law, and shall not delay case handling or disrupt work. The following acts are strictly prohibited:

(1) Intentionally delaying case filing, service, or transfer;

(2) Without justifiable reasons or without approval, seriously exceeding the time limit for case trial or enforcement.

Where the above provisions are violated, the judge shall be given a warning up to a major demerit; if serious consequences are caused, the judge shall be dismissed or given a punishment of demotion or above. Where removal from office or expulsion is imposed, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post.

Article 12 Judges shall serve the people wholeheartedly, and shall not use their powers to seek personal gain for themselves or others. The following acts are strictly prohibited:

(1) Borrowing money, housing, or items such as transportation or communication tools from parties or their authorized persons;

(2) Accepting benefits provided by parties or their authorized persons in purchasing goods, decorating housing, or other aspects;

(3) Accepting cash, negotiable securities, or payment instruments given by supervisory or subordinate departments and their staff, lower-level courts and their staff, law firms and lawyers, or other units and individuals related to the exercise of their powers;

(4) Using their powers to seek illegitimate benefits for their spouses, children, or other relatives.

Where the above provisions are violated, the judge shall be given a warning up to a major demerit; if the circumstances are serious, the judge shall be dismissed or given a punishment of demotion or above. Where removal from office or expulsion is imposed, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post.

Article 13 Judges shall strive to perform their own duties well, and shall not engage in for-profit business activities. The following acts are strictly prohibited:

(1) Engaging in business or running enterprises individually;

(2) Holding concurrent posts in economic entities;

(3) Engaging in paid intermediary activities;

(4) Serving concurrently as a lawyer or legal advisor.

Where the above provisions are violated, the judge shall be given a warning up to a major demerit; if a bad influence is caused, the judge shall be dismissed or given a punishment of demotion or above. Where removal from office or expulsion is imposed, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post.

Article 14 Judges shall strictly observe trial discipline, and shall not meet privately with parties or their agents, or accept invitations for meals or gifts from parties or their agents. The following acts are strictly prohibited:

(1) Meeting privately with parties, their agents ad litem, defenders, or authorized persons;

(2) Participating in banquets, entertainment, fitness, or tourism activities paid for by parties, their agents ad litem, defenders, authorized persons, or intermediary institutions;

(3) Accepting gifts from parties, their agents ad litem, defenders, or authorized persons.

Where the above provisions are violated, the judge shall be given a warning up to a major demerit; if a bad influence is caused, the judge shall be dismissed or given a punishment of demotion or above. Where removal from office or expulsion is imposed, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post.

Article 15 Judges shall be of good conduct, abide by professional ethics, and shall not commit other illegal or disciplinary violations.

Where this Article is violated, the judge shall be given a warning up to a major demerit; if the circumstances are serious, the judge shall be dismissed or given a punishment of demotion or above. Where removal from office or expulsion is imposed, the appointing and removing authority shall be requested to remove the judge from his or her post.

Article 16 Where these Provisions are violated and a punishment of removal from office is imposed, the judge's salary and rank shall also be reduced; if a crime is constituted, criminal liability shall be pursued according to law.

Article 17 Where a judge violates the above provisions, and the leader of the unit or department where the judge works bears serious responsibility for dereliction of duty, or conceals, fails to investigate, or covers up disciplinary or legal violations occurring in the unit or department, the leader's responsibility shall be pursued in accordance with relevant provisions.

Article 18 Where a judge violates these Provisions and it is necessary to request the appointing and removing authority to remove the judge from his or her post and dismiss the judge, the political department shall handle the matter; where an administrative disciplinary sanction is to be imposed, the supervision department shall handle the matter.

The authority and procedures for disposition and punishment shall be handled in accordance with relevant provisions.

Article 19 Where enforcement officers, court clerks, judicial police, judicial鉴定 personnel, or other court staff violate these Provisions, they shall be dealt with by reference to these Provisions.

Article 20 These Provisions shall come into force on the date of issuance.

June 18, 2003
